  "id": "DrugBank_DB01177 - MONDO_18437",
  "verbalization": "Idarubicin is associated with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through a series of indications that ultimately link it to AML with Npm1 somatic mutations. The evidence suggests that Idarubicin is indicated for AML with specific chromosomal abnormalities, such as T(6;9)(p23;q34) or Inv3(p21;q26.2), both of which are associated with Npm1 mutations. These mutations are commonly found in AML cases, and the shared indication with Glasdegib, a drug that targets Npm1 mutations, further supports a mechanistic link. The presence of multiple pathways that ultimately point to Npm1 mutations indicates that Idarubicin may be repurposed for AML with Npm1 mutations through a mechanism involving the targeting of these mutations by related drugs. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Idarubicin to be used in the treatment of AML with Npm1 mutations, based on the shared indications and the underlying biological relationship between the mutations and the drugs.",
